WEBVTT

00:00.130 --> 00:02.000
- [Instructor] So, we're told that this

00:02.000 --> 00:04.600
is the graph of function
f right over here.

00:04.600 --> 00:08.260
And then they tell us that
function g is defined as

00:08.260 --> 00:11.340
g of x is equal to one third f of x.

00:11.340 --> 00:13.090
What is the graph of g?

00:13.090 --> 00:14.540
And if we were doing this on Khan Academy,

00:14.540 --> 00:16.720
this is a screenshot from our mobile app,

00:16.720 --> 00:17.940
it has multiple choices,

00:17.940 --> 00:19.680
but I thought we could
just try to sketch it.

00:19.680 --> 00:23.100
So pause this video, maybe in your mind,

00:23.100 --> 00:25.520
imagine what you think the graph
of g is going to look like,

00:25.520 --> 00:27.320
or at least how you would tackle it.

00:28.250 --> 00:31.670
All right, so g of x is
equal to one third f of x.

00:31.670 --> 00:36.300
So, for example, we can
see here that f of three

00:36.300 --> 00:38.520
is equal to negative three.

00:38.520 --> 00:41.870
So, g of three should be one third that,

00:41.870 --> 00:43.713
so it should be negative one.

00:44.780 --> 00:47.920
Likewise, so, g of three
would be right over there,

00:47.920 --> 00:51.980
and likewise, g of negative
three, what would that be?

00:51.980 --> 00:54.590
Well, f of negative three is three,

00:54.590 --> 00:57.820
so g of negative three is
going to be one third that,

00:57.820 --> 01:00.850
or it's going to be equal to one.

01:00.850 --> 01:03.630
F of zero is zero, one
third of that is still zero,

01:03.630 --> 01:06.230
so g of zero is still going
to be right over there.

01:06.230 --> 01:09.860
And we know that's going to
happen there and there as well,

01:09.860 --> 01:11.360
and so we already have a sense of what

01:11.360 --> 01:13.360
this graph is going to look like.

01:13.360 --> 01:16.930
The function g is going
to look something like,

01:16.930 --> 01:18.050
something like this.

01:18.050 --> 01:19.880
I'm just connecting the dots

01:19.880 --> 01:21.520
and they did give us some dots

01:21.520 --> 01:23.630
that we can use as reference points,

01:23.630 --> 01:27.020
so the graph of g is going
to look something like this.

01:27.020 --> 01:28.790
It gets a little bit flattened out

01:28.790 --> 01:32.730
or a little bit squooshed
or smooshed a little bit

01:32.730 --> 01:33.620
to look something like that

01:33.620 --> 01:35.590
and you would pick the
choice that looks like that.

01:35.590 --> 01:36.840
Let's do another example.

01:38.410 --> 01:42.897
So, here we are told
this the graph of f of x

01:42.897 --> 01:45.420
and it's defined by this expression.

01:45.420 --> 01:46.860
What is the graph of g of x,

01:46.860 --> 01:48.380
and g of x is this.

01:48.380 --> 01:50.630
So pause this video and
think about it again.

01:51.720 --> 01:53.930
All right, now the key realization is,

01:53.930 --> 01:56.700
is it looks like g of x is,

01:56.700 --> 01:58.900
if you were to take
all the terms of f of x

01:58.900 --> 02:01.340
and multiply it by two,

02:01.340 --> 02:02.210
or at least if you were to multiply

02:02.210 --> 02:03.970
the absolute value by two,

02:03.970 --> 02:06.560
and then if you were to multiply
this negative two by two.

02:06.560 --> 02:11.560
So it looks like g of x
is equal to two times,

02:11.680 --> 02:13.830
two times f of x.

02:13.830 --> 02:16.060
And we could even set
up a little table here,

02:16.060 --> 02:18.560
this is another of the way
that we can think about it.

02:18.560 --> 02:23.110
We can think about x, we
can think about f of x,

02:23.110 --> 02:24.700
and now we can think about g of x,

02:24.700 --> 02:26.530
which should be two times that.

02:26.530 --> 02:29.610
So we can see that when
x is equal to zero,

02:29.610 --> 02:31.920
f of x is equal to one,

02:31.920 --> 02:34.100
so g of x should be equal to two

02:34.100 --> 02:37.130
because it's two times f of x.

02:37.130 --> 02:39.190
So g of x is going to be equal to--

02:39.190 --> 02:42.450
Or g of zero, I should say,
is going to be equal to two.

02:42.450 --> 02:45.550
What about when at x equals,

02:45.550 --> 02:47.690
we'll say when x equals three.

02:47.690 --> 02:51.060
When x equals three, f
of x is negative two.

02:51.060 --> 02:54.040
G of x is going to be two times that,

02:54.040 --> 02:55.512
'cause it's two times f of x,

02:55.512 --> 02:57.150
so it's going to be negative four.

02:57.150 --> 02:59.820
So, g of x, or I should say g of three

02:59.820 --> 03:01.000
is going to be negative four.

03:01.000 --> 03:02.920
It's going to be right over there.

03:02.920 --> 03:05.560
And then maybe let's think
about one more point.

03:05.560 --> 03:10.560
So, f of five is equal to zero.

03:10.610 --> 03:12.330
G of five is going to be two times that,

03:12.330 --> 03:13.870
which is still going to be equal to zero,

03:13.870 --> 03:15.570
so it's going to be right over there.

03:15.570 --> 03:18.940
And so the graph is going
to look something like this,

03:18.940 --> 03:20.950
I'm just really just connecting,

03:20.950 --> 03:22.440
I'm just connecting the dots,

03:22.440 --> 03:24.380
trying to draw some straight lines.

03:24.380 --> 03:26.230
It's going to look something like this,

03:26.230 --> 03:28.170
you can see it's kind of stretched

03:28.170 --> 03:30.240
in the vertical direction.

03:30.240 --> 03:31.820
So, if you were doing
this on Khan Academy,

03:31.820 --> 03:32.653
it'd be multiple choice,

03:32.653 --> 03:34.890
you'd look for the graph
that looks like that.

03:34.890 --> 03:36.423
Let's do a few more examples.

03:37.710 --> 03:41.010
So, here we're given a function g

03:41.010 --> 03:44.150
is a vertically scaled version of f.

03:44.150 --> 03:47.910
So we can see that g is a
vertically scaled version of f.

03:47.910 --> 03:50.980
The functions are graphed
where f is a solid

03:50.980 --> 03:51.813
and g is dashed.

03:51.813 --> 03:52.840
Yeah, we see that.

03:52.840 --> 03:56.320
What is the equation of g in terms of f?

03:56.320 --> 03:58.670
So, pause this video and
try to think about it.

03:59.700 --> 04:01.900
Well, the way that I would
tackle this is once again,

04:01.900 --> 04:03.260
let's do it with a table

04:03.260 --> 04:06.150
and let's see the
relationship between f and g.

04:06.150 --> 04:08.930
So, this column is x,

04:08.930 --> 04:12.350
this column is f of x,

04:12.350 --> 04:15.685
and then this column is g of x.

04:15.685 --> 04:16.960
I'll make another column right over here.

04:16.960 --> 04:18.840
And so, let's see some interesting points.

04:18.840 --> 04:22.310
So, when, actually, I could pick zero,

04:22.310 --> 04:23.880
but zero is maybe less interesting

04:23.880 --> 04:25.270
than this point over here.

04:25.270 --> 04:28.600
So, this is when x is
equal to negative three.

04:28.600 --> 04:31.360
F of negative three is negative three.

04:31.360 --> 04:33.220
What is g of negative three?

04:33.220 --> 04:34.920
It looks like it is negative nine.

04:36.360 --> 04:39.900
When f is, when x is zero,

04:39.900 --> 04:42.520
f of zero is negative two.

04:42.520 --> 04:44.390
What is g of zero?

04:44.390 --> 04:47.820
It is equal to two negative six.

04:47.820 --> 04:50.550
And so we already see a pattern forming.

04:50.550 --> 04:53.840
Whatever f is, g is three times that.

04:53.840 --> 04:56.580
Whatever f is, g is three times that.

04:56.580 --> 04:59.460
And so we don't even actually
need these big columns,

04:59.460 --> 05:05.640
but we can see that g of x is
equal to three times f of x.

05:05.640 --> 05:08.201
So that is the equation
of g in terms of f.
